  "full_response": "A typical Kubernetes cluster consists of several components working together to provide container orchestration. The main components of a Kubernetes cluster are the control plane, worker nodes, and pods. Here is an overview of the and the role of each component:\n\n1. Control Plane (Master Node(s)): The control plane is responsible for managing the overall state and behavior of the Kubernetes cluster.\nComponents within the control plane include:\n- Kube-apiserver: This is the primary component of the Kubernetes control. It acts as the front-end for the Kubernetes API and serves as the communication gateway for all requests made by users or applications to the Kubernetes cluster.\n- Kube-scheduler: The kube-scheduler is responsible for placing Pods onto Nodes. It looks at the wishes and requirements specified by the user in the PodSpec and assigns the Pod to a Node that can fulfill those requirements.\n- Kube-controller-manager: The kube-controller-manager runs controller processes, which are workers that regulate the state and operation of the Kubernetes cluster. Controllers handle tasks such as replicating objects, performing a backup, ensuring that current state matches the intended state.\n- Etcd: Etcd is a distributed key-value store used by Kubernetes as its backing store for all cluster data. It acts as the stable storage for all cluster data and state, including objects, state, policies, and namespaces.\n\n2. Worker Nodes: Worker nodes are responsible for running the containerized applications that make up the application services.\nComponents within the worker node include:\n- Kubelet: The kubelet is responsible for managing the lifecycle and state of the containers on a particular node. It ensures the containers are running as expected and maintains their state.\n- Container Runtime: The container runtime is the underlying software responsible for running containers on nodes. Kubernetes supports several container runtimes, including Docker, containerd, and CRI-O.\n- Kube-proxy: The kube-proxy part of the kubelet and it serves as a network proxy which allows for service-oriented networking. It watches the Kubernetes API server for traffic to Services, DRLs, and Endpoints; and routes the network traffic to the correct Pod instances based on the Configurations defined.\n\n3. Pods: Pods are the smallest deployable units in Kubernetes. Pod contains one or more containers. The Pod will house together a single instance of a workload along with its associated resources. In a Pod, one container can share a common IP address and can share storage along with other levels of isolation.\nA Helm Chart: A Helm Chart is an independent package",
